            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the border, then the Spanish for to become independent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: el relevo",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el relevo — \"the replacement, the handover; the relay\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Su hija tomó el relevo en la tienda. Her daughter took over at the shop."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tomar el relevo — to take over. In sport, los relevos is the relay."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "relevo is built on relevar 'to relieve, to replace' (EtymDB), which comes from Latin relevāre 'to lift up, to lighten', built on levāre 'to raise' (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
